'''Alausa''' (a-lâ′sä), ''n.'' Same as ''Alosa''.

'''alb'''<sup>1</sup> (alb), ''n.'' [< ME. ''albe'', < AS. ''albe'', < ML. ''alba'' (sc. ''vestis'', garment), fem. of L. ''albus'', white: see ''aube'', the French form.] '''1.''' In the ''Rom. Cath. Ch.'' (and in many Anglican churches), a white linen robe, with tight sleeves, worn at the celebration of the eucharist under the chasuble, cope, or dalmatic by the officiating priest and his assistants. {{smaller|It reaches to the feet, and is bound around the waist by a girdle called the ''alb-cord''. Usually it is ornamented at the edges and wrists with embroidery or lace-work. The alb was formerly the common dress of the clergy. Colored albs have been used in the service of the English Church. The corresponding garment in the Greek Church is the stoicharion (which see).}}
{{smaller block|A white ''albe'' plain with a vestment or cope.
{{right|''Book of Common Prayer'' (1549).}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Each priest adorn’d was in a surplice white;
The bishops donn’d their ''albs'' and copes of state.</poem>
{{right|''Fairfax'', Tasso, ii. 4.}}}}
'''2.''' In the ''early church'', a white garment worn from the Saturday before Easter until the first Sunday after Easter by the newly baptized.

Formerly also written ''alba'', ''albe''.

'''Apparels of the alb''', {{smaller|square pieces of embroidery in colors or precious orphrey-work sewed or otherwise fastened upon the alb, commonly in six places: much used between the eleventh and sixteenth centuries.}}

'''alb'''<sup>2</sup> (alb), ''n.'' [Turk.] A small Turkish coin, nearly equal in value to a cent.

'''alba''' (al′bä), ''n.'' [NL. (sc. ''substantia''), fem. of L. ''albus'', white, used as a noun.] White fibrous nerve-tissue, as distinguished from the gray or cellular.
{{smaller block|The ''alba'' constitutes the columns of the myelon, etc.}}

